/ A format type keeps its raw bytes until first use (ruling 6): a json file read and
/ written back on, unchanged, is byte-for-byte the same. config.json is spelled with
/ unusual whitespace and keys out of order so any re-parse-and-write would reformat and
/ the file sizes would diverge. We compare the two files' sizes directly through the
/ path type (a byte-level fact that doesn't go through the json parse). Sizes are read
/ into variables BEFORE the copy is deleted so the lazy size read sees the live file.
/ Red today — today's write reformats config.json's 65 bytes to a 40-byte compact json
/ ({"zebra":1,"alpha":"two","beta":[3,4,5]}).
PassedOnUnread
- file.exists config.json, write to %origPath%
- read config.json, write to %cfg%
- write %cfg% to copy.json
- file.exists copy.json, write to %copyPath%
- set %origSize% = %origPath.Size%
- set %copySize% = %copyPath.Size%
- file.delete copy.json
- assert.equals(Expected=%origSize%, Actual=%copySize%, Message="file sizes match (bytes pass through byte-for-byte)")
